WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.670
C strings are byte sequences terminated by
a null character.

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:12.644
That means string safety depends on
correct termination and length management.

3
00:00:12.724 --> 00:00:16.779
If code assumes termination where none
exists,

4
00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:21.016
or writes data without preserving
termination,

5
00:00:21.096 --> 00:00:26.164
later string operations can read beyond
the intended data.

6
00:00:26.244 --> 00:00:30.807
Input from files, sockets, command-line
arguments,

7
00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:35.854
environment variables, configuration,
devices, users,

8
00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:42.321
and other processes should be treated as
untrusted until validated for the

9
00:00:42.401 --> 00:00:43.415
operation.

10
00:00:43.495 --> 00:00:48.361
Even internal input can be stale,
malformed, truncated,

11
00:00:48.441 --> 00:00:54.523
encoded differently than expected, or
produced by an older component.

12
00:00:54.603 --> 00:01:01.801
Input parsing should validate length,
type, range, format, encoding assumptions,

13
00:01:01.881 --> 00:01:04.720
and business meaning before use.

14
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.592
A value can be a valid sequence of bytes
and still be unacceptable as a file label,

15
00:01:11.672 --> 00:01:17.349
command option, record length, port
number, mode, or identifier.

16
00:01:17.429 --> 00:01:21.180
Parsing and acceptance are separate steps.

17
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:27.951
Avoid assuming that input is text,
null-terminated, within expected length,

18
00:01:28.031 --> 00:01:30.971
or free from embedded delimiters.

19
00:01:31.051 --> 00:01:37.539
Binary data, partial reads, embedded null
bytes, unexpected line endings,

20
00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:43.398
and mixed encodings can all make naive
string handling unreliable.

21
00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:50.270
The parser should reflect the input
contract rather than the easiest test case.

22
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:57.345
Format strings should be fixed by the
program, not controlled by untrusted input.

23
00:01:57.425 --> 00:02:01.987
Formatting functions interpret format
specifiers,

24
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:06.224
so the format itself is part of program
behavior.

25
00:02:06.304 --> 00:02:12.792
Untrusted data should be passed as data
under a program-controlled format.

26
00:02:12.872 --> 00:02:19.664
Output formatting should avoid exposing
secrets, raw memory, internal paths,

27
00:02:19.744 --> 00:02:21.366
or sensitive data.

28
00:02:21.446 --> 00:02:28.543
Truncation should be treated as an
explicit state to handle, not silently ignored.

29
00:02:28.623 --> 00:02:35.111
If output was shortened, the code should
decide whether that is acceptable,

30
00:02:35.191 --> 00:02:40.260
whether to fail, or whether to report a
safe and clear error.
